WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.150 --> 00:00:05.250
The idea that our planet’s continents drift
around the globe, periodically glomming together

00:00:05.250 --> 00:00:08.180
and breaking apart, is at least 200 years
old.

00:00:08.180 --> 00:00:12.410
But most geologists didn’t believe it until
the 1960’s, when mounting evidence made

00:00:12.410 --> 00:00:16.160
it clear that the Earth’s crust is broken
up into fragments, and that those fragments,

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.560
called tectonic plates, are moving.

00:00:18.560 --> 00:00:23.109
And these days we directly track that motion
– with millimeter precision – from space.

00:00:23.109 --> 00:00:27.160
The common, simplified explanation for why
tectonic plates are moving is that they’re

00:00:27.160 --> 00:00:31.310
carried along on currents in the upper mantle,
the slowly flowing layer of rock just below

00:00:31.310 --> 00:00:32.540
Earth’s crust.

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:37.160
Converging currents drive plates into each
other; diverging currents pull them apart.

00:00:37.160 --> 00:00:41.580
This is mostly true; hot mantle rock rises
from the core and moves along under the crust

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.650
until it grows cool and heavy and sinks back
down again.

00:00:44.650 --> 00:00:47.680
But the plates aren't just passively riding
these conveyer-belt-like currents around like

00:00:47.680 --> 00:00:49.600
a bunch of suitcases at the baggage claim.

00:00:49.600 --> 00:00:53.670
They can’t be, because some of the plates
are moving faster than the currents underneath

00:00:53.670 --> 00:00:54.670
them.

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:58.430
For example, the Nazca plate – a chunk of
ocean crust off the west coast of South America

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:03.190
– is cruising eastward at about 10cm per
year, while the mantle underneath it oozes

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:04.799
along at just five.

00:01:04.799 --> 00:01:09.120
Neither tectonic plates nor luggage can move
faster than the belt they’re riding on unless

00:01:09.120 --> 00:01:11.690
something else is helping to push or pull
them along.

00:01:11.690 --> 00:01:14.600
And some of Earth’s plates, it turns out,
are pulling themselves.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.000
&nbsp;When an ocean plate collides with another
ocean plate or a plate bearing the thick crust

00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:23.290
of continental landmasses, the thinner of
the two plates bends and slides under the

00:01:23.290 --> 00:01:24.290
other.

00:01:24.290 --> 00:01:27.580
As the edge of the seafloor sinks into the
mantle, it pulls on the plate behind it, the

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:32.040
same way a chain dangling further and further
off a table will eventually start to slide.

00:01:32.040 --> 00:01:35.700
The bigger the sunken portion of the plate
becomes, the harder it pulls and the faster

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:37.660
the remaining plate behind it moves.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.540
You can find where this is happening by looking
at google earth – the incredibly deep, narrow

00:01:41.540 --> 00:01:46.220
ocean trenches visible off the coasts of some
continents and island chains mark the creases

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:50.650
formed as ocean crust plunges downward, bending
the edge of its neighbor in the process.

00:01:50.650 --> 00:01:54.940
What’s more, those chunks of seafloor are
actually helping to drive convection in the

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:56.640
mantle beneath them.

00:01:56.640 --> 00:02:00.780
Sunken slabs of ocean crust block flowing
rock from moving further sideways, forcing

00:02:00.780 --> 00:02:03.200
it to turn downward and sink.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.530
Eventually those slabs get too heavy and break
off, plunging slowly toward the core and creating

00:02:07.530 --> 00:02:10.750
a suction force that pulls mantle material
along behind it.

00:02:10.750 --> 00:02:15.540
So, in some ways, seafloor crust really is
more like part of the conveyor belt than something

00:02:15.540 --> 00:02:16.829
riding on top of it.

00:02:16.829 --> 00:02:19.249
The continents, on the other hand, are baggage.

